## The Opportunity for Winfred

The advisory-tier fee transparency space is **completely empty**. The key insight: nobody has reframed fees as a function of *financial outcomes delivered* rather than a transaction cost. Ohmyhome competes on cheap; PLB justifies expensive. Neither speaks to Winfred's ICP — HDB upgraders and restructuring couples making $1.2–1.6M moves who need strategic advice.

**Three content angles flagged:**
1. **"What does your 1% buyer advisory fee actually cover?"** — scope-of-work breakdown mapped to The Move (M1–M5)
2. **"The advisory fee conversation nobody is having"** — reframe 1% as outcomes (CPF savings, ABSD avoided, equity unlocked) not transaction cost
3. **"Why I will sometimes tell you not to proceed"** — address the commission misalignment conflict honestly; nobody is doing this

**Longer-term positioning move:** A public scope-of-work page on the site that explains what the engagement covers and what Winfred *doesn't* take on — no rate card needed, just the actual advisory practice made visible. That's a moat competitors can't fake.

